; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What every gear_mesh variant shares, stated once: the cell, the arm, the
; physics, the cameras, the episode count and the routine. Each .task beside
; this file inherits it and says only what makes it a tier -- its gears, its
; goal and its step budget.
;
; The mat and the board are NOT here, deliberately. Every goal clause names the
; board, and a document is read on its own by the gauntlet and the exporter --
; both typecheck the .task alone, without merging this file -- so the fixtures
; a document's clauses refer to stay declared in the document that refers to
; them. What is shared is scenery nothing names, and this suite has none.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (suite gear_mesh)

  (:language Mesh a train of involute spur gears onto an assembly board and
             press the start button -- the board is scored on whether every
             gear then turns, on a single-arm Panda.)

  (:staging
    ; bench_075 with a light-grey top (and the mat is pale too, assets/layout.py):
    ; the parts are small and metal, and on the palette's dark slab the frame
    ; read as a dark grey room. Same geometry, same surface height.
    (:scene bench_075_light)
    ; The 2F-85, welded on in place of the Franka hand. `panda_robotiq` is the
    ; same arm at the same pedestal with its own hand cut out, and the hand is
    ; the Menagerie 2f85.xml as physics: two driven revolutes, two four-bar
    ; loops closed by <connect> equalities, box pads at mu 0.7 with priority 1
    ; (so pad-on-tooth contacts take the pad's friction, as MuJoCo does), the
    ; tooth prisms met under multiccd like every other mesh pair here.
    ;
    ; MEASURED ON THIS PAIRING (2026-09-09, gearmesh-easy-single-01):
    ; * The pads swing on the linkage, so how far they sit past the tool centre
    ;   is not one number: 5.9 mm at full open, 11.5 mm at the 67 mm pre-open a
    ;   20-tooth gear gets, 15-16 mm closed on it. `robot.get_gripper` reports
    ;   the live figure as `tip_reach_m`; the graph reads it rather than a
    ;   stated constant, and never calibrates by pushing the pads into the mat
    ;   -- a vertical push at the fingertips folds the passive four-bar (the
    ;   adaptive mode: coupler and spring link off their stops, follower to its
    ;   limit) and the tool centre travels 19 mm further before anything stalls.
    ; * Closed on a gear at the hand's own gains (ke 20, kd 1 per driver) the
    ;   pads hold it with about 10 N, 0.8 mm into the tooth tips, and an 80 mm
    ;   lift loses 0.7 mm; at ke 200 the force is 50-60 N and the lift slips
    ;   9 mm; at ke 1000 it is 380-450 N, the gear tilts 20 degrees and is flung
    ;   at the lift; at the 4000/100 the Franka hand ran here the jaws slam shut
    ;   in ten steps and throw the gear across the bench before they touch it.
    ;   So no gripper-actuation stanza: a torque gain per radian is not a force
    ;   gain per metre, and the hand's own is what the spec records.
    (:embodiment panda_robotiq)
    (:arms 1)
    (:control-mode joint_position)
    ; Tooth against tooth is a mesh-mesh contact two millimetres across, so the
    ; multi-point manifold is the whole task and the contact gap is coupled to
    ; the tooth profile rather than free. See tasks/gear_mesh/staging.py.
    (:physics gear_mesh_960hz@v1)
    (:cameras gear_mesh_views@v2)
    ; Twelve layouts: where the loose gears wait, and how they are turned. The
    ; first randomised sampler in this corpus -- assets/layout.py, which also
    ; says what a sampler structurally cannot vary here (a size, or a count).
    (:episodes (:count 12) (:seed 0) (:sampler assets.layout:scatter_and_clock))
    ; usbc's, polish's and box_decant's stiffened servos, for their reason:
    ; lowering a bore over a peg with 0.3 mm of radial clearance is task-space
    ; positioning under contact, and at the MJCF's own gain the TCP lags
    ; further than the fit is wide.
    (:arm-actuation (:ke 1600) (:kd 70))
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; A routine, and one graph. The routine is open-loop by construction -- every
  ; target is a peg whose position the board derives, so there is nothing to
  ; poll. The graph is what the routine could not be: it finds the board and the
  ; gears with the overhead camera alone (SAM3 through the shared tool bundle,
  ; --perception vision --no-privileged --renderer tiled), and it retries a
  ; gear that lands on a tooth. Declared here so every variant, drawn or
  ; hand-written, accepts it.
  ; The Franka hand this suite was first written against, as a host rather
  ; than the native pairing. It stands and the graph runs on it, but the finger
  ; gains it was measured under (ke 4000, kd 100 per metre; the hand's own 1500
  ; let a carried gear slip 6 mm) cannot be stated on a host row, so its
  ; numbers recorded for the round-1/2 evaluations are the last taken before the switch.
  (:hosts
    (:host (:embodiment panda)
           (:graphs gap_perception)
           (:notes "compiles; the round-1/2 evaluation numbers
                    were taken on it under (:gripper-actuation (:ke 4000) (:kd 100)),
                    which a host row cannot carry; unmeasured at the hand's own gain")))

  (:artifacts
    (:routine "routine:RoutineGearMesh")
    (:graph gap_perception "graphs/gap_perception")
    ; The house room with the lab panel turned up: the bench and mat are dark
    ; fixture greys and read dark under a path tracer calibrated to the tiled
    ; frame. Only the path tracer reads the key. See assets/look.py.
    (:render "assets.look:GEAR_MESH_LOOK")
  )

  ; The tiers, easiest first, then the drawn levels. Each document's :objects
  ; say its stations in order; assets/layout.py's STATIONS table restates them,
  ; because the poses each document records are written from it.
  ;
  ; THE DRAWN LEVELS ARE A DISTRIBUTION, NOT A LAYOUT. gearmesh-<level>-<family>-NN
  ; is one draw of tools/build_gear_scenes.py: a one-, two- or three-station
  ; board (difficulty.py says which level that is) whose plate centre, chain
  ; turn, gear spots and clocks were drawn at the seed the document's header
  ; states, and kept only if a hand can work the layout. One episode each --
  ; the recorded draw is the scene -- so a score on the level is a score on the
  ; draw rather than on a hundred seeds of one arrangement. The rows of record
  ; are assets/variants.json; the block below is written from them, and the
  ; loader refuses a tree where the two disagree.
  (:variants pair train quad quad_seeded
             gearmesh-easy-single-01 gearmesh-easy-single-02 gearmesh-easy-single-03 gearmesh-easy-single-04
             gearmesh-easy-single-05 gearmesh-easy-single-06 gearmesh-easy-single-07 gearmesh-easy-single-08
             gearmesh-easy-single-09 gearmesh-easy-single-10 gearmesh-easy-single-11 gearmesh-easy-single-12
             gearmesh-easy-single-13 gearmesh-easy-single-14 gearmesh-easy-single-15 gearmesh-easy-single-16
             gearmesh-easy-single-17 gearmesh-easy-single-18 gearmesh-easy-single-19 gearmesh-easy-single-20
             gearmesh-easy-single-21 gearmesh-easy-single-22 gearmesh-easy-single-23 gearmesh-easy-single-24
             gearmesh-easy-single-25 gearmesh-easy-single-26 gearmesh-easy-single-27 gearmesh-easy-single-28
             gearmesh-easy-single-29 gearmesh-easy-single-30 gearmesh-easy-single-31 gearmesh-easy-single-32
             gearmesh-easy-single-33 gearmesh-easy-single-34 gearmesh-easy-single-35 gearmesh-easy-single-36
             gearmesh-easy-single-37 gearmesh-easy-single-38 gearmesh-easy-single-39 gearmesh-easy-single-40
             gearmesh-easy-pair-01 gearmesh-easy-pair-02 gearmesh-easy-pair-03 gearmesh-easy-pair-04
             gearmesh-easy-pair-05 gearmesh-easy-pair-06 gearmesh-easy-pair-07 gearmesh-easy-pair-08
             gearmesh-easy-pair-09 gearmesh-easy-pair-10 gearmesh-easy-pair-11 gearmesh-easy-pair-12
             gearmesh-easy-pair-13 gearmesh-easy-pair-14 gearmesh-easy-pair-15 gearmesh-easy-pair-16
             gearmesh-easy-pair-17 gearmesh-easy-pair-18 gearmesh-easy-pair-19 gearmesh-easy-pair-20
             gearmesh-easy-pair-21 gearmesh-easy-pair-22 gearmesh-easy-pair-23 gearmesh-easy-pair-24
             gearmesh-easy-pair-25 gearmesh-easy-pair-26 gearmesh-easy-pair-27 gearmesh-easy-pair-28
             gearmesh-easy-pair-29 gearmesh-easy-pair-30 gearmesh-easy-pair-31 gearmesh-easy-pair-32
             gearmesh-easy-pair-33 gearmesh-easy-pair-34 gearmesh-easy-pair-35 gearmesh-easy-pair-36
             gearmesh-easy-pair-37 gearmesh-easy-pair-38 gearmesh-easy-pair-39 gearmesh-easy-pair-40
             gearmesh-easy-pair-41 gearmesh-easy-pair-42 gearmesh-easy-pair-43 gearmesh-easy-pair-44
             gearmesh-easy-pair-45 gearmesh-easy-pair-46 gearmesh-easy-pair-47 gearmesh-easy-pair-48
             gearmesh-easy-pair-49 gearmesh-easy-pair-50 gearmesh-easy-pair-51 gearmesh-easy-pair-52
             gearmesh-easy-pair-53 gearmesh-easy-pair-54 gearmesh-easy-pair-55 gearmesh-easy-pair-56
             gearmesh-easy-pair-57 gearmesh-easy-pair-58 gearmesh-easy-pair-59 gearmesh-easy-pair-60
             gearmesh-medium-train-001 gearmesh-medium-train-002 gearmesh-medium-train-003 gearmesh-medium-train-004
             gearmesh-medium-train-005 gearmesh-medium-train-006 gearmesh-medium-train-007 gearmesh-medium-train-008
             gearmesh-medium-train-009 gearmesh-medium-train-010 gearmesh-medium-train-011 gearmesh-medium-train-012
             gearmesh-medium-train-013 gearmesh-medium-train-014 gearmesh-medium-train-015 gearmesh-medium-train-016
             gearmesh-medium-train-017 gearmesh-medium-train-018 gearmesh-medium-train-019 gearmesh-medium-train-020
             gearmesh-medium-train-021 gearmesh-medium-train-022 gearmesh-medium-train-023 gearmesh-medium-train-024
             gearmesh-medium-train-025 gearmesh-medium-train-026 gearmesh-medium-train-027 gearmesh-medium-train-028
             gearmesh-medium-train-029 gearmesh-medium-train-030 gearmesh-medium-train-031 gearmesh-medium-train-032
             gearmesh-medium-train-033 gearmesh-medium-train-034 gearmesh-medium-train-035 gearmesh-medium-train-036
             gearmesh-medium-train-037 gearmesh-medium-train-038 gearmesh-medium-train-039 gearmesh-medium-train-040
             gearmesh-medium-train-041 gearmesh-medium-train-042 gearmesh-medium-train-043 gearmesh-medium-train-044
